adjudication.rationale:
In `reauthenticationOfUserWithoutPasskey()` the test checks absence of the WebAuthn form via:
```java
try {
  MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
  fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
} catch (Exception nsee) {
  // expected
}
```
However, `driver.findElement(...)` throws (typically `NoSuchElementException`) when the element is absent, so the `assertThat(..., nullValue())` is never reached in the intended (absent) case. The test is therefore relying on the exception side-effect rather than asserting the condition, making it fragile and potentially masking unexpected failures (it catches any `Exception`).

In PasskeysUsernamePasswordFormTest.reauthenticationOfUserWithoutPasskey(), the diff introduces assertions for absence of the WebAuthn form using a broad try/catch:

```java
try {
  MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
  fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
} catch (Exception nsee) {
  // expected
}
```

This pattern can mask unexpected WebDriver/Selenium exceptions (it catches any Exception, not specifically NoSuchElementException), so the test could pass for the wrong reason if findElement fails due to a transient driver/timing issue rather than true absence. That’s a real, diff-grounded test-quality concern, but not a product defect.
